ck","year":1982,"weight":5,"yt":"ZbuvnCKp2qU","src":"musthear","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":126751190489920,"name":"RYM Top — R&B — Smooth Soul","rank":139,"scaruffi":null},{"id":168502129438589,"name":"RYM Top — Pop — Adult Contemporary","rank":53,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":2}},{"id":181136895707532,"type":"song","name":"Heartbreaker","artist":"Dionne Warwick","year":1983,"weight":4,"yt":"9xHQsMYosao","src":"charts,singles","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":120682792121633,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1983","rank":80,"scaruffi":null},{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","rank":80,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":2}},{"id":233007124836261,"type":"song","name":"Then Came You (N/A)","artist":"Dionne Warwick","year":null,"weight":4,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[{"name":"For the Record - The 70s","n":1}],"lists":[],"n_lists":0}}],"members":[],"member_of":[{"id":163131400309059,"type":"artist","name":"Dionne and Friends","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":8,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":253869975365644,"type":"artist","name":"The Spinners (joint performance on chart hit)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":49756993232774,"type":"artist","name":"The Shirelles (as replacement singer)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"genres":[],"album_genres":[{"id":242048506390872,"type":"genre","name":"Pop Soul","n":7},{"id":50337978407967,"type":"genre","name":"Smooth Soul","n":5},{"id":247802226836647,"type":"genre","name":"Brill Building","n":2},{"id":9343125461845,"type":"genre","name":"Adult Contemporary","n":2},{"id":163536058891268,"type":"genre","name":"Traditional Pop","n":1},{"id":30122583811636,"type":"genre","name":"Soul","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":153468859939986,"name":"Las cantantes de 1966","book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","start":1961,"end":1967,"desc":"La primavera de 1966 trajo discos que respiraban apertura, y algunos de los más imaginativos los cantaban mujeres. «Walkin’ My Cat Named Dog» de Norma Tanega (febrero de 1966), producido por Herb Bernstein para Bob Crewe, mezclaba folk y bajo Motown, filosofía y ritmo go-go: una mujer que pasea sola y sin miedo por Nueva York, tan moderna y alusiva como Dylan. Tanega, pintora licenciada y camarera de campamento en los Catskills, se vio de pronto en una gira de seis semanas como única mujer (se cambiaba en un establo) y después en Londres, donde vivió con Dusty Springfield. Nancy Sinatra, con Lee Hazlewood, mezclaba Las Vegas, Los Ángeles, fantasía sadomaso y venganza femenina. Tras los Beatles, el pop británico había quedado dominado por hombres y las cantantes iban juntas en un mismo reportaje: Helen Shapiro no sobrevivió al Merseybeat; Cilla Black (Epstein, versiones de Bacharach/David), Sandie Shaw (una chica Biba descalza, fría y dueña de su imagen, que en «Nothing Comes Easy» canta a una acosadora) y Marianne Faithfull (elegida por su aspecto por Andrew Loog Oldham y arrojada a las giras) eran pioneras por el simple hecho de actuar.","color":"#38bdf8","family":"Pop de estudio: Spector, Beach Boys, Meek","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#cantantes-1966"},{"id":22485426612515,"name":"Dusty Springfield","book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","start":1963,"end":1970,"desc":"Tras los Springfields (folk alegre: «Island of Dreams», «Silver Threads and Golden Needles»), en Estados Unidos descubrió «Don’t Make Me Over» de Dionne Warwick y «Tell Him» de las Exciters y buscó el sonido Spector en «I Only Want to Be with You» (1963), que la lanzó como la primera de la nueva generación de cantantes. Amiga de Vicki Wickham, era habitual de Ready Steady Go!, promotora incansable de Motown y una presencia musical que hacía los arreglos vocales con Johnny Franz («una gran ceñuda»). Su máscara de ojos de panda escondía una timidez extrema; colapsos, una gira sudafricana cortada por negarse a cantar ante público segregado, icono gay sin poder decirlo y etiquetada de «bachelor girl». «I Just Don’t Know What to Do with Myself» fue su primera obra maestra. «You Don’t Have to Say You Love Me» (marzo de 1966), adaptación de un tema de San Remo de Pino Donaggio con letra de Wickham y Simon Napier-Bell, cantada en la escalera del estudio de Philips, fue número 1 a finales de abril y la consagró como diva trágica.","color":"#38bdf8","family":"Pop de estudio: Spector, Beach Boys, Meek","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#dusty-springfield"},{"id":233389633737411,"name":"Warhol, la Factory y el pop de 1966","book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","start":1962,"end":1968,"desc":"En la primavera de 1966 Warhol era el artista más famoso de Estados Unidos y ya no hacía arte en el sentido tradicional: había anunciado en 1965 que dejaba la pintura por el cine, la gente y los entornos totales. La Factory de la calle 47 Este era a la vez cadena de producción de serigrafías, plató y escenario abierto; un espejo de la sociedad neoyorquina donde lo que esta despreciaba (drag queens, yonquis, chaperos, perturbados) podía volverse superstar. Sus cuadros devolvían a América sus valores (sexo, muerte, crimen, fama, dinero, producción en masa) y sus series de desastres el regodeo mediático en la muerte. Obrero gay de Pittsburgh con una rabia de outsider, se convirtió en estrella de rock en el caos de su retrospectiva de Filadelfia (octubre de 1965). Gloria Steinem lo puso en el centro de la cultura pop en Life y Susan Sontag hizo de lo camp una obsesión; Batman, A Clockwork Orange, el caso de los asesinatos de Tucson y Capote alimentaban el aire de violencia que él recortaba de las revistas. Con la anfetamina el ambiente se volvió paranoico y cruel; Danny Williams, su amante y autor de las luces del EPI, desapareció en julio. Durante 1966, sin pretenderlo, «se convirtió en América».","color":"#e5e7eb","family":"Vanguardia: Warhol, Velvet, free jazz, arte","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#warhol-factory"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":5513657154759,"artist":"Dionne Warwick","artist_id":73209732617463,"title":"Don’t Make Me Over","year":1962,"ess":0,"yt":"dDblF-J6qvY","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"Dusty Springfield","scene_id":22485426612515,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#dusty-springfield","chapters":["Mayo: Walkin’ My Cat Named Dog: la mística de la feminidad y la independencia femenina"],"pages":[[1,130],[1,510]]},{"id":187789686998627,"artist":"Dionne Warwick","artist_id":73209732617463,"title":"A House Is Not a Home","year":1964,"ess":0,"yt":"Mk7CNHHIuH4","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"Warhol, la Factory y el pop de 1966","scene_id":233389633737411,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#warhol-factory","chapters":["Junio: I’ll Be Your Mirror: la Velvet Underground y la América de Warhol"],"pages":[[1,151],[1,510]]},{"id":41750751216177,"artist":"Dionne Warwick","artist_id":73209732617463,"title":"A Message to Michael","year":1966,"ess":1,"yt":"GAzJZrxZN2w","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"Las cantantes de 1966","scene_id":153468859939986,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#cantantes-1966","chapters":["Discografía: Los singles de cada mes de 1966"],"pages":[[1,394]]},{"id":180338840482402,"artist":"Dionne Warwick","artist_id":73209732617463,"title":"Trains and Boats and Planes","year":1966,"ess":1,"yt":"nuBumHuouug","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"Las cantantes de 1966","scene_id":153468859939986,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#cantantes-1966","chapters":["Discografía: Los singles de cada mes de 1966"],"pages":[[1,396]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":124,"text":"…hit. Black usually covered American material. Her first #1 was ‘Anyone Who Had a Heart’, a huge US hit by prime Burt Bacharach/Hal David interpreter Dionne Warwick. Although she had a tendency to bellow like a foghorn during a song’s climax, Cilla released several fine singles, including an early cover of Randy…"},{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":130,"text":"…this quite clearly, because you always remember the First Time. I was sitting in the Capitol Motel in Nashville when I heard “Don’t Make Me Over” by Dionne Warwick. I actually sat down very hard because it was different to anything I had heard before. The other record was “Tell Him” by the Exciters, which I…"},{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":132,"text":"…Lesley Gore’s ‘You Don’t Own Me’. In 1964, she focused on songs by Burt Bacharach and Hal David. Her first cover was of ‘Wishin’ and Hopin’’ – a Dionne Warwick flipside in early 1963. Both Cilla Black and Sandie Shaw had hits with covers of Warwick-sung Bacharach– David songs that year, so Dusty decided to get ahead of the game by visiting the writers themselves in New York. She came away with…"},{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":151,"text":"…soundtrack, along with Billy Name’s opera records. The next year, he remembers working at the Factory to Lesley Gore singing ‘You Don’t Own Me’ and Dionne Warwick’s ‘A House Is Not a Home’. This formed an aural collage, a clashing sound environment. Remembering his routine for 1964, Warhol remarked how after a…"},{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":394,"text":"…Smash We the People, ‘My Brother the Man’, Hotline Percy Sledge, ‘When a Man Loves a Woman’, Atlantic Jim Reeves, ‘Distant Drums’, RCA US Dionne Warwick, ‘A Message to Michael’, Scepter Phil Upchurch Combo, ‘You Can’t Sit Down (Part 1)’, Sue UK Mitch Ryder and the Detroit Wheels, ‘Little Latin Lupe…"},{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":396,"text":"…Nowhere’, Reprise The Surfaris, ‘Wipe Out’, DOT reissue Los Bravos, ‘Black Is Black’, Decca Herman’s Hermits, ‘This Door Swings Both Ways’, Columbia Dionne Warwick, ‘Trains and Boats and Planes’, Scepter The Roosters, ‘One of These Days’, Progressive Sounds of America We the People, ‘Mirror of Your Mind’,…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Declined Humans Album Participation","body":"Dionne Warwick declined to participate in the Gorillaz album \"Humans,\" citing \"some kind of religion\" as her reason. \"those who declined to participate included Morrissey, and Dion Warwick, who had some kind of religion.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Gorillaz - A History","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iVdTCqpd798"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Scopatone Involvement","body":"Dionne Warwick was among the artists who produced films for the Scopatone machines. These machines were a form of video jukebox popular in the mid-1960s, offering short, often color, musical films in public venues. \"The Sedacca, Bobby V, Dion Warwick, and a few others got into the Scopatone business.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Rise and Fall and Future of the Music Video - Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lqd6IbUJ7tg"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Session Singer and Replacement for The Shirelles","body":"Dionne Warwick served as a session singer for Scepter Records, and was employed as a replacement singer for The Shirelles' live shows when original members were on maternity leave. At the time, she was being produced by Burt Bacharach for Scepter. \"The singer who replaced them for those shows was a session singer who Bakerak was producing for Septa, named Dianne Warwick.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 89: \"Will You Love Me Tomorrow?\" by the Shirelles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7Kns2HGcuSg"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Hasty Pudding Woman of the Year (1970)","body":"Won.","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q10294045"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"New Jersey Hall of Fame (2013)","body":"Won.","year":2013,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q14705795"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Marian Anderson Award (2017)","body":"Won.","year":2017,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6761828"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Kennedy Center Honors (2023)","body":"Won.","year":2023,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1738793"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Ellis Island Medal of Honor","body":"Won.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q5365910"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"star on Hollywood Walk of Fame","body":"Won.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q17985761"}]}],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":2229033237715,"type":"album","name":"Presenting Dionne Warwick","list_id":106824060559178,"list":"RYM Top — R&B — Pop Soul","src":"musthear","rank":51,"year":1963,"note":null,"yt":"PSXpKMmEBXg","scaruffi":null},{"id":78964909516770,"type":"song","name":"Walk On By","work_year":1964,"list_id":161507989025637,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1964","src":"singles","rank":37,"year":1964,"note":"","yt":"vsGsCvJWEo8","scaruffi":null},{